"Autumn is matched to metal and the lung in the five-element framework, and it marks the turn from the outward-facing first half of the year to the inward-facing second half. Where spring encourages growth and summer encourages full expansion, autumn's classic advice is about gathering in — the Yellow Emperor's Inner Classic recommends going to bed earlier and rising with the sunrise, keeping the mind calm and the emotions settled rather than scattered, matching a season whose defining image is falling leaves drawing inward and downward rather than a spring shoot reaching out. Autumn's defining physical challenge, in this framework, is dryness — the season's dominant excess from an earlier unit — and it's matched specifically to the lung, an organ this course has already connected to skin health and the body's outer defenses. Dry air is traditionally thought to injure the lung's own moisture first, showing up as a dry cough, dry skin, and a dry nose or throat that arrives with the season rather than from any illness. The classic response is to eat moistening foods and avoid activities, like excessive sweating, that dry the body further at exactly the time of year it can least afford it. The emotion matched to autumn is grief, matched to the lung by the same logic covered in the organs unit, and the seasonal advice treats this pairing as reason for gentleness rather than alarm — a normal autumn tendency toward quiet reflection, even mild melancholy, isn't necessarily a problem to fix, but a pattern this framework expects and makes room for, the same way it expects spring's outward energy and winter's quiet. Reading a season's mood as part of its physiology, rather than as separate from it, is the through-line this entire unit has been building toward.": "ead5a25c8d2f",
